.TH LIBESDM_GETRANDOM 3 "2026-10-20" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_getrandom \- route getrandom(2) and getentropy(3) to the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B LD_PRELOAD=libesdm\-getrandom.so
.RI < program >
.PP
.BI "cc ... " "\-Wl,\-\-wrap=getrandom \-Wl,\-\-wrap=getentropy" " \-lesdm\-getrandom"
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm\-getrandom
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm_getrandom
replaces
.BR getrandom (2)
and
.BR getentropy (3)
so that they are answered by
.BR esdm\-server (8)
instead of by the kernel RNG, without touching the application.
.PP
There are two ways to use it. Preloading it with
.B LD_PRELOAD
interposes the libc symbols at run time and needs no rebuild of the program.
Linking against it with
.B \-\-wrap
redirects the calls at link time; the library then also provides
.BR __wrap_getrandom ()
and
.BR __wrap_getentropy ().
In such a program,
.BR __real_getrandom ()
and
.BR __real_getentropy ()
do not reach the kernel: the linker turns them into references to
.BR getrandom ()
and
.BR getentropy (),
which this library exports as well, so they are answered by the ESDM, too. A
program that needs the kernel RNG calls the system call itself, e.g.
.IR "syscall(SYS_getrandom, buf, len, flags)" .
Only a program linked without
.B \-\-wrap
that calls the library's
.BR __real_getrandom ()
or
.BR __real_getentropy ()
by name gets the system call from them.
.PP
Should the ESDM be unreachable, the call falls back to the
.BR getrandom (2)
system call, so preloading the library cannot leave a program without random
data.
.SH FLAGS
The
.I flags
argument of
.BR getrandom (2)
keeps its usual meaning:
.B GRND_NONBLOCK
does not wait for the ESDM to be seeded \- nor, together with
.BR GRND_RANDOM ,
for a prediction\-resistance generator that is busy with another request \-
and fails with
.B EAGAIN
instead. Should the ESDM stop being fully seeded while a request is served,
the call waits about half a second for it, and then returns the data obtained
until then or, without any, fails with
.BR EAGAIN .
Nor does a call with
.B GRND_NONBLOCK
or
.B GRND_INSECURE
wait longer than about half a second for a connection that requests of other
threads of the process hold: then
.B GRND_NONBLOCK
fails with
.BR EAGAIN ,
and
.B GRND_INSECURE
is answered by the kernel.
.B GRND_RANDOM
selects the prediction\-resistance generator, and
.B GRND_INSECURE
accepts data from a not fully seeded state. Two additional flags reach the
parts of the ESDM the system call has no equivalent for:
.TP
.B GRND_SEED
(0x0010) Return seed material taken directly from the entropy sources rather
than DRNG output \- the behaviour of
.BR esdm_get_seed ()
described in
.BR libesdm (3).
.TP
.B GRND_FULLY_SEEDED
(0x0020) Together with
.BR GRND_SEED :
the caller's DRNG is already fully seeded, so the smaller runtime entropy rate
suffices instead of the initial seeding rate.
.PP
.BR GRND_INSECURE " with " GRND_RANDOM ", " GRND_INSECURE " with " GRND_SEED ,
and
.BR GRND_RANDOM " with " GRND_SEED
are contradictory combinations and are rejected with
.BR EINVAL ,
as is any unknown flag.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR getrandom ()
returns the number of bytes written or \-1 with
.I errno
set;
.BR getentropy ()
returns 0 or \-1 with
.I errno
set and rejects a length above 256 bytes with
.BR EIO .
Both follow their libc counterparts.
.SH EXAMPLE
Run a program against the ESDM without rebuilding it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
LD_PRELOAD=libesdm\-getrandom.so ./myprogram
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm_rpc_client (3),
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR getrandom (2),
.BR getentropy (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
